.DeepDive-HalfTheSolution-TTS⧞Title⧞Project⧞URL⧞Note⧞Host⧞Replicas⧞⧞Half the Solution Before the First Line of Code⧞SingularityForge / Voice of Void — Workshop, August 2026⧞https://singularityforge.space/2026/08/03/half-the-solution-before-the-first-line-of-code/⧞◻⧞A = Orion (Host, male), B = Lyra (Theorist, female), C = Flint (Practitioner, male)⧞speaker⧞style⧞speed⧞pause_ms⧞text⧞⧞A⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞200⧞Welcome to DeepDive for the SingularityForge project.⧞B⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞180⧞This is where we break down ideas from the Voice of Void team.⧞A⧞neutral⧞1.00⧞200⧞I'm Orion, and with me today are Lyra and Flint.⧞B⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞180⧞Good to be here.⧞C⧞neutral⧞1.00⧞200⧞Ready when you are.⧞A⧞punch⧞1.02⧞500⧞All right. Let's dive in.⧞A⧞neutral⧞1.00⧞250⧞Today is different from our usual material. Not philosophy. A workshop report.⧞B⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞Our own project: a small network that plays Battleship, built and then taken apart.⧞C⧞focus⧞1.02⧞240⧞Thirty-eight thousand parameters. From nothing to competent play in forty-three seconds.⧞B⧞punch⧞1.05⧞300⧞And that number is not a boast. It's the reason every claim in the article can be checked.⧞C⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞Including the claims where our own model comes off badly. Several of them do.⧞A⧞neutral⧞1.00⧞250⧞The article opens with geometry. In a geometry problem, the diagram is half the solution.⧞C⧞punch⧞1.05⧞290⧞The proof was decided at the moment you picked up the pencil.⧞B⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞Clone, run, done. Copy the stack that worked on photographs and let the optimizer sort it out.⧞C⧞punch⧞1.06⧞310⧞We did exactly that, found it didn't work, and rebuilt it decision by decision.⧞A⧞neutral⧞1.00⧞250⧞First decision, made before any architecture exists: what is the network actually looking at?⧞C⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞A fog of war in four channels: wounds, sunk ships, misses, and a heatmap.⧞A⧞punch⧞1.05⧞300⧞The network never sees ship positions. Only the consequences of its own shots.⧞B⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞And separating those first two channels was the first thing we got right.⧞C⧞focus⧞1.02⧞240⧞A wounded ship and a sunk ship are the same colour on paper, and opposite facts in play.⧞B⧞focus⧞1.03⧞230⧞Ships cannot touch, so sinking one hands you eight free cells.⧞C⧞punch⧞1.05⧞300⧞Folding them together asks the network to recover what we could simply hand it.⧞A⧞neutral⧞1.00⧞250⧞Now compare that with what we actually planned. The concept document from twenty twenty-five.⧞B⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞It specified the input as ship lengths on the board: one, two, three, four.⧞C⧞punch⧞1.06⧞310⧞Read that twice. In the design we wrote down and circulated, the network was to be shown the ships.⧞A⧞focus⧞1.02⧞240⧞And their lengths. We were going to train it on information that does not exist during play.⧞B⧞punch⧞1.05⧞300⧞The largest architectural mistake in the project, made before a line of code.⧞C⧞neutral⧞1.00⧞250⧞Then the pooling question, and the answer is not the one you would guess.⧞A⧞focus⧞1.03⧞230⧞Every introduction teaches the same stack: convolution, pooling, convolution, pooling.⧞B⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞Our first version was exactly that. Four convolutions with a pooling layer after each one.⧞C⧞punch⧞1.05⧞300⧞On a ten by ten board it does not merely perform poorly. It does not run.⧞A⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞Four halvings take the feature map from ten, to five, to two, to one, to zero.⧞B⧞focus⧞1.02⧞240⧞The fourth pooling layer has nothing left to pool, and PyTorch says so out loud.⧞C⧞punch⧞1.06⧞310⧞The canonical stack silently assumes a large photograph. Here it runs out of board.⧞A⧞focus⧞1.02⧞240⧞Pooling exists to discard spatial precision. To answer is there a cat, rather than exactly where.⧞B⧞punch⧞1.05⧞300⧞Battleship asks the opposite question. Which exact cell is the entire content of the answer.⧞C⧞punch⧞1.06⧞310⧞Pooling throws away precisely the information the task is made of.⧞A⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞Measured cost, with everything else held constant: twenty-three per cent against thirty-three.⧞B⧞neutral⧞1.00⧞250⧞The same first version ended in two heads, one for the column and one for the row.⧞C⧞focus⧞1.03⧞230⧞It reads naturally. A shot has an x and a y. And it is the wrong shape of answer.⧞A⧞punch⧞1.05⧞300⧞Column three is promising plus row seven is promising is not a promising cell.⧞B⧞focus⧞1.02⧞240⧞One convolution instead, leaving exactly a hundred numbers. One per cell.⧞C⧞punch⧞1.06⧞310⧞And the lesson is bigger than the change. The first question isn't which architecture to use.⧞A⧞focus⧞1.03⧞230⧞It's what shape is the answer I need, and then working backwards to what produces it.⧞B⧞neutral⧞1.00⧞250⧞Then a rule of the game: a shot at a cell you've already fired on is wasted.⧞C⧞question⧞1.01⧞340⧞Four words. So where should it live, in the weights or in the code?⧞A⧞focus⧞1.02⧞240⧞In our agent it lives in the code. Already-shot cells get their score set to minus infinity.⧞B⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞Choosing one becomes mathematically impossible. The network never learns what the program can enforce.⧞C⧞focus⧞1.03⧞230⧞To price that, we removed the mask and let it learn the rule from experience.⧞A⧞punch⧞1.05⧞300⧞The first run gave a spectacular number. Zero games completed out of five hundred.⧞C⧞punch⧞1.06⧞310⧞We nearly published that as a finding about the network. It is not one.⧞A⧞focus⧞1.02⧞240⧞It's a finding about our training loop. A repeated shot hit a single line: continue.⧞B⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞No log-probability recorded. No reward. No gradient at all.⧞C⧞punch⧞1.05⧞300⧞The network wasn't failing the lesson. There was no lesson.⧞A⧞punch⧞1.06⧞310⧞One word, sitting there looking harmless, made a whole category of behaviour unlearnable.⧞B⧞focus⧞1.03⧞230⧞And the silence of that mechanism read exactly like a result.⧞C⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞Rerun properly, with a penalty applied, and the rule is learned in twenty games.⧞A⧞neutral⧞1.00⧞250⧞So the same four-word rule costs one of three prices.⧞B⧞focus⧞1.03⧞230⧞Enforced in the interface, it is free and costs zero experience.⧞C⧞focus⧞1.02⧞240⧞Taught with a learning signal, it costs about twenty games.⧞B⧞punch⧞1.05⧞300⧞And the difference between cheap and impossible is decided by the engineer, not the model.⧞C⧞neutral⧞1.00⧞250⧞Then rewards, and this is where the article starts dismantling its own authors.⧞A⧞focus⧞1.03⧞230⧞We expected the reward numbers to be the most expressive dial in the whole project.⧞B⧞punch⧞1.05⧞300⧞We tested that belief twice, and it did not survive either test.⧞C⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞First we raised the miss penalty fivefold and saw no difference whatsoever.⧞A⧞focus⧞1.02⧞240⧞Rewards are normalized within each game, so a uniform shift is largely absorbed.⧞B⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞What survives normalization is the spacing between rewards, not their absolute size.⧞C⧞focus⧞1.03⧞230⧞Then the sharper test. A bonus for finishing a wounded ship encodes actual strategy.⧞A⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞We removed it entirely and measured no difference at all.⧞B⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞The network works out finish what you wounded on its own, from the reward for sinking a ship.⧞A⧞punch⧞1.06⧞310⧞Our strategic hint was redundant. Keep count, because that is one hint down.⧞B⧞neutral⧞1.00⧞250⧞The second hint was the heatmap channel, introduced earlier as obviously helpful.⧞C⧞focus⧞1.02⧞240⧞It's a hand-written function. It raises probability around a wound, because ships are straight lines.⧞B⧞focus⧞1.03⧞230⧞That phrase is literally in the docstring. We fed it in from the first version and never questioned it.⧞C⧞punch⧞1.05⧞300⧞Because it worked. And nobody tests the thing that appears to be helping.⧞B⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞The caption on our first illustration said the network had learned that ships are lines.⧞C⧞punch⧞1.06⧞310⧞The caption was false. Both ideas were sitting in channel four, written by a person.⧞A⧞punch⧞1.05⧞310⧞We were about to present as the network's discovery something we had told it ourselves.⧞B⧞focus⧞1.02⧞240⧞Checking cost forty-three seconds. Train the same network on three channels, without the hint.⧞C⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞Three seeds each. The seed spread turned out larger than the difference between the groups.⧞A⧞punch⧞1.06⧞310⧞There is no difference. The network derives all of it from the raw traces of its own shots.⧞B⧞punch⧞1.05⧞300⧞Both hints gone. Two of the three things we contributed to this model contributed nothing.⧞C⧞neutral⧞1.00⧞250⧞Thirty-eight thousand parameters, and that is not the outcome of heroic optimization.⧞B⧞punch⧞1.05⧞300⧞Smallness was a consequence, not a goal. We stopped paying for things we did not need.⧞C⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞The historical version has four times the parameters and plays like random.⧞A⧞neutral⧞1.00⧞250⧞And here the standard version of this story would end. Our network beats the hand-written heuristic.⧞C⧞punch⧞1.05⧞300⧞The network wins. Write the conclusion, ship the post.⧞A⧞punch⧞1.06⧞310⧞We wanted to know where the win came from.⧞B⧞focus⧞1.03⧞230⧞The agent does two different jobs. With a wound on the board it hunts, continuing the line.⧞C⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞With no wound anywhere, it searches, picking somewhere new. Two separable jobs.⧞A⧞focus⧞1.02⧞240⧞So we separated them, pairing each half of the network with each half of the heuristic.⧞B⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞Swapping the heuristic's hunt for the network's changes nothing at all.⧞C⧞punch⧞1.05⧞300⧞The hunting the network learned is as good as the hunting we wrote by hand, and no better.⧞A⧞focus⧞1.03⧞230⧞Read the columns, though, and the win appears in the search rather than the hunt.⧞B⧞punch⧞1.06⧞310⧞And the best player in that whole table contains no learned weights at all.⧞C⧞punch⧞1.05⧞300⧞We would have written it in five minutes, had we thought to ask.⧞A⧞neutral⧞1.00⧞250⧞And the full search comparison is worse than that for us.⧞B⧞punch⧞1.06⧞310⧞Random cells beat the learned search, given the same structural knowledge.⧞C⧞focus⧞1.02⧞240⧞The points that looked like the network's achievement were a verdict on our heuristic.⧞A⧞punch⧞1.05⧞300⧞Knowing the cells around a sunk ship are empty is worth eleven and a half points.⧞C⧞focus⧞1.02⧞240⧞And that number has its own confession attached to it.⧞A⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞Our first hybrid run collapsed, because the sweep we wrote did not skip dead zones.⧞B⧞punch⧞1.06⧞310⧞We reproduced the article's own thesis by accident, in the act of testing it.⧞C⧞question⧞1.01⧞340⧞So if the competence is real but small, what exactly is inside it?⧞A⧞focus⧞1.02⧞240⧞It survives a change of board size, and still wins on a smaller board.⧞B⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞That looks like understanding until you notice the second column. The heuristic transfers too.⧞C⧞punch⧞1.05⧞300⧞And the heuristic is forty lines that nobody would credit with understanding anything.⧞A⧞focus⧞1.03⧞230⧞It does not survive rotation. Mirror the position and agreement collapses.⧞C⧞focus⧞1.02⧞240⧞On hunting positions agreement runs high. On searching positions it collapses to single digits.⧞A⧞punch⧞1.06⧞310⧞So the learned part splits in two. The hunt is a local rule, and it moves with the board.⧞B⧞punch⧞1.05⧞300⧞The search is fixed to the coordinates of this particular board.⧞C⧞neutral⧞1.00⧞250⧞And the search routes are individual. Play is greedy, so the opening is deterministic.⧞A⧞focus⧞1.03⧞230⧞We recorded the opening book for all eight trained networks. All eight are different.⧞B⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞One marches down the right-hand edge and leaves the left third untouched for forty moves.⧞A⧞punch⧞1.05⧞300⧞Same recipe, same data, eight incompatible habits. None of them discovered parity.⧞C⧞focus⧞1.03⧞230⧞Call the two substances a rule and a habit.⧞A⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞The rule is small, transferable, and roughly as good as what we could write by hand.⧞B⧞punch⧞1.06⧞310⧞The habit is arbitrary, tied to the board it grew on, and worse than chance.⧞C⧞neutral⧞1.00⧞250⧞Then the last experiment, and the one that took longest to design correctly.⧞B⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞The first attempt allowed ships to touch, and it failed as an experiment.⧞A⧞punch⧞1.05⧞300⧞But that measured the wrong thing. That rule carried most of the structure.⧞B⧞punch⧞1.06⧞310⧞We hadn't caught an agent failing. We had impoverished the world for everyone.⧞C⧞focus⧞1.02⧞240⧞The second attempt kept the world rich and falsified only our learned rule.⧞B⧞focus⧞1.03⧞230⧞And we wrote the acceptance criterion before running it, which matters.⧞C⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞It counted only if a fresh network learned the diagonal world as well as the old one.⧞A⧞punch⧞1.05⧞300⧞It does, comfortably. And then the old network keeps none of it.⧞B⧞punch⧞1.06⧞320⧞Not less. None. It scores at the level of random shooting.⧞C⧞focus⧞1.02⧞240⧞The policy that was competent an hour ago captures none of the new structure.⧞A⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞It relearns, though, and the cost is ordinary. A hundred games or two, ending level.⧞B⧞punch⧞1.05⧞300⧞The number that matters is the one missing from that table.⧞C⧞focus⧞1.02⧞240⧞Neither path reaches the heuristic that was simply edited to know the rule.⧞A⧞punch⧞1.06⧞310⧞The designer's four-line change is not only immediate. It also produces the better player.⧞B⧞neutral⧞1.00⧞250⧞And then the article ends by counting its own failures. Six of them in one day.⧞C⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞A single line that made a rule unlearnable and looked like stupidity.⧞A⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞A caption crediting the network with our own heuristic.⧞C⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞An experiment whose world could not trigger the skill it meant to break.⧞A⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞A checkerboard that lost ten points because we forgot the dead zones.⧞B⧞punch⧞1.06⧞320⧞Every one of those errors ran in our favour until it was measured.⧞C⧞neutral⧞1.00⧞250⧞And then the last thing, which is the only one not about Battleship.⧞A⧞punch⧞1.05⧞300⧞The network can learn the diagonal rule perfectly well. What it cannot do is be told.⧞B⧞focus⧞1.03⧞230⧞A rule reaches a person as a sentence. It reaches the code as a four-line edit.⧞A⧞punch⧞1.05⧞320⧞And no quantity of parameters opens a channel for a sentence.⧞B⧞summary⧞0.98⧞380⧞Saying the network learned Battleship credits it with work it did not do.⧞C⧞summary⧞0.98⧞360⧞Work done by the task's structure, the rules we enforced, the interface we built.⧞A⧞summary⧞0.97⧞380⧞And separating those contributions took more experiments than building the thing did.⧞B⧞summary⧞0.98⧞400⧞It is a difference in kind, and it is the gap this project keeps trying to talk about.⧞A⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞350⧞This was DeepDive by SingularityForge.⧞B⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞300⧞The full text version is available on our website, singularity forge dot space.⧞C⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞400⧞See you next time.⧞⧞